## Solution

### Setup: Parabolic coordinates

Parabolic coordinates $(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$ with
$$x=\alpha\beta\cos\gamma,\quad y=\alpha\beta\sin\gamma,\quad z=\tfrac{\alpha^{2}-\beta^{2}}{2}.$$

Scale factors (from $\mathbf r=(\alpha\beta\cos\gamma,\alpha\beta\sin\gamma,\tfrac{\alpha^{2}-\beta^{2}}{2})$):
$$h_\alpha=\sqrt{\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2}},\quad h_\beta=\sqrt{\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2}},\quad h_\gamma=\alpha\beta.$$

### Identifying the surface

On $\mathcal P$: $x^{2}+y^{2}-1=2z$. Using $x^{2}+y^{2}=\alpha^{2}\beta^{2}$ and $2z=\alpha^{2}-\beta^{2}$:
$$\alpha^{2}\beta^{2}-1=\alpha^{2}-\beta^{2}\;\Longrightarrow\;\alpha^{2}\beta^{2}-\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2}-1=0\;\Longrightarrow\;(\beta^{2}-1)(\alpha^{2}+1)=0.$$
Since $\alpha^{2}+1>0$, we get $\beta^{2}=1$, i.e. **$\beta=1$** (taking $\beta\ge 0$). The $z$-range $-0.5\le z\le 4$ gives $z=\tfrac{\alpha^{2}-1}{2}\in[-\tfrac12,4]\Rightarrow\alpha^{2}\in[0,9]\Rightarrow\alpha\in[0,3]$, with $\gamma\in[0,2\pi)$.

### (a) Flux in parabolic coordinates

The vector field has components
$$G_\alpha=(\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2})\cos\gamma,\qquad G_\beta=\frac{1}{\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2}},\qquad G_\gamma=0.$$

**Orientation.** The outward (convex-side) normal of the paraboloid $f=x^{2}+y^{2}-2z-1=0$ is $\nabla f\propto(x,y,-1)$. On $\beta=1$, $(x,y,-1)=(\alpha\cos\gamma,\alpha\sin\gamma,-1)$, which is exactly the direction of $\hat h_\beta=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2}}}(\alpha\cos\gamma,\alpha\sin\gamma,-\beta)$. Hence the outward normal is $+\hat h_\beta$.

The oriented surface element on the coordinate surface $\beta=\text{const}$ is
$$d\mathbf S = h_\alpha\,h_\gamma\,\hat h_\beta\,d\alpha\,d\gamma = \alpha\beta\sqrt{\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2}}\;\hat h_\beta\,d\alpha\,d\gamma.$$

Therefore
$$\mathbf G\cdot d\mathbf S = G_\beta\,h_\alpha h_\gamma\,d\alpha\,d\gamma = \frac{1}{\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2}}\,\alpha\beta\sqrt{\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2}}\,d\alpha\,d\gamma = \frac{\alpha\beta}{\sqrt{\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2}}}\,d\alpha\,d\gamma.$$

Setting $\beta=1$, $\alpha\in[0,3]$, $\gamma\in[0,2\pi]$:
$$\Phi=\int_{0}^{2\pi}\!\!\int_{0}^{3}\frac{\alpha}{\sqrt{\alpha^{2}+1}}\,d\alpha\,d\gamma = 2\pi\Big[\sqrt{\alpha^{2}+1}\Big]_{0}^{3}=2\pi\big(\sqrt{10}-1\big).$$

$$\boxed{\Phi=2\pi\big(\sqrt{10}-1\big).}$$

### (b) Verification via cylindrical coordinates

In cylindrical coordinates $(r,\phi,z)$: $x=r\cos\phi,\;y=r\sin\phi$. The surface is $r^{2}-1=2z$, i.e. $z=\tfrac{r^{2}-1}{2}$, with $r\in[0,3]$ (from $z\in[-\tfrac12,4]$) and $\phi\in[0,2\pi)$. On the surface $\beta=1$ we have $r=\alpha\beta=\alpha$ and $\phi=\gamma$.

Parametrize $\mathbf r(r,\phi)=(r\cos\phi,r\sin\phi,\tfrac{r^{2}-1}{2})$. Then
$$\mathbf r_r\times\mathbf r_\phi = r(-r\cos\phi,-r\sin\phi,1),$$
which points inward (toward the axis). The **outward** element is
$$d\mathbf S_{\text{out}} = r(r\cos\phi,r\sin\phi,-1)\,dr\,d\phi.$$

**Express $\mathbf G$ on the surface.** Using $\hat h_\alpha=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2}}}(\beta\cos\gamma,\beta\sin\gamma,\alpha)$ and $\hat h_\beta=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2}}}(\alpha\cos\gamma,\alpha\sin\gamma,-\beta)$, with $\beta=1$, $\alpha=r$, $\gamma=\phi$:
$$\mathbf G = \sqrt{r^{2}+1}\cos\phi\,(\cos\phi,\sin\phi,r) + \frac{1}{(r^{2}+1)^{3/2}}(r\cos\phi,r\sin\phi,-1).$$

**Dot with $d\mathbf S_{\text{out}}$:**

First term:
$$\sqrt{r^{2}+1}\cos\phi\,(\cos\phi,\sin\phi,r)\cdot r(r\cos\phi,r\sin\phi,-1) = \sqrt{r^{2}+1}\cos\phi\cdot r\big[r\underbrace{(\cos^{2}\phi+\sin^{2}\phi)}_{=1}-r\big]=0.$$

Second term:
$$\frac{1}{(r^{2}+1)^{3/2}}(r\cos\phi,r\sin\phi,-1)\cdot r(r\cos\phi,r\sin\phi,-1)=\frac{r}{(r^{2}+1)^{3/2}}\big(r^{2}+1\big)=\frac{r}{\sqrt{r^{2}+1}}.$$

Hence
$$\mathbf G\cdot d\mathbf S_{\text{out}}=\frac{r}{\sqrt{r^{2}+1}}\,dr\,d\phi,$$
and
$$\Phi=\int_{0}^{2\pi}\!\!\int_{0}^{3}\frac{r}{\sqrt{r^{2}+1}}\,dr\,d\phi = 2\pi\Big[\sqrt{r^{2}+1}\Big]_{0}^{3}=2\pi\big(\sqrt{10}-1\big).$$

This **agrees exactly** with part (a). $\blacksquare$

